{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>The end of all flesh has come before me.</b> The day of death is called 'the end of all flesh' — it has come before me and complains that it has no remembrance among all living creatures, and no one has taken it to heart. For on this account the Holy One, blessed be He, waited through the seven days of mourning for Methuselah, for perhaps seeing the wise die, the living will take it to heart — for this is the desired ultimate purpose of mourning and eulogy. For the robber who amasses wealth unjustly — had he remembered the day of death, that in the midst of his days he must leave it, he would not have robbed from others; but rather he surely thinks that he hoards forever, as it says (Psalms 49:12) ״קִרְבָּם בָּתֵּימוֹ לְעוֹלָם״ and so forth. And I have with me a wondrous explanation of the entire psalm, which speaks of the matter of those who amass money and their delusion; but since in this commentary I have not come to elaborate on matters that are not germane to the pericope, therefore I shall not cite it here — only this verse alone, which says ״קִרְבָּם בָּתֵּימוֹ לְעוֹלָם, מִשְׁכְּנֹתָם לְדוֹר וָדוֹר״. First it says ״בָּתֵּימוֹ״ and afterward ״מִשְׁכְּנֹתָם״; first it says ״לְעוֹלָם״ and afterward ״לְדוֹר וָדוֹר״. And the correct interpretation of this is that the wicked man who builds for himself in this world a sturdy building with spacious upper stories of marble — surely ״קִרְבָּם״, that is, their inner thought, that ״בָּתֵּימוֹ לְעוֹלָם״ — that the house of stone will stand forever. And if you say: what benefit does he have from this, that the house will stand forever, after he himself will not dwell and reside in it forever — does he not know that his end is to die? — of this it says ״מִשְׁכְּנֹתָם לְדוֹר וָדוֹר״, that he gives a blessing of peace to himself: even if he himself will not dwell in it, nevertheless his sons and his sons' sons, generation after generation, will dwell in it. And if you say: what satisfaction is it to its owners if his sons dwell in it while he dwells in the grave? — of this it says ״קָרְאוּ בִשְׁמוֹתָם עֲלֵי אֲדָמוֹת״, that he proceeds under a great delusion, saying that when his sons give their sons and daughters their own names, then the name of the dead will be called upon his inheritance and will not be cut off — and it seems to him as though he were alive, and the matter of yibbum will prove it. From the side of this delusion, even the day of death will not turn them back for the better; therefore ״קֵץ כָּל בָּשָׂר בָּא״ — it comes and complains before me, as aforementioned." "And there are those who say: ״קֵץ כָּל בָּשָׂר״ — this is the angel of death, the Destroyer, and from soul to flesh he consumes and brings their end near, and he comes before me and seeks to be given into his hand whoever is destined for death. And the Holy One, blessed be He, said: if I give permission to the Destroyer, he will not distinguish between righteous and wicked; therefore ״וְהִנְנִי מַשְׁחִיתָם״ — I myself, in my own person. And so that Noah too would not be included in the destruction, therefore ״עֲשֵׂה לְךָ תֵּבַת״ and so forth. A hint to the matter: ״קֵץ כָּל בָּשָׂר״ — the final letters of the words ״כָּל בָּשָׂר״ give the numerical value 231 (רל״א), equal to the numerical value of ״מַלְאָךְ סַמָּאֵל״, together with the eight letters that are in these two words."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {0 "The phrase ״קָרְאוּ בִשְׁמוֹתָם עֲלֵי אֲדָמוֹת״ is cited from Psalms 49:12, not written with an explicit verse address in the source."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:27:32.554231579Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}